WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.710 --> 00:00:05.610
We may not know much about the Captain Marvel
movie yet but we do know this '90s-set superhero

00:00:05.610 --> 00:00:09.900
adventure will star Oscar winner Brie Larson
as the first female in the Marvel Universe

00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:11.820
to lead her own standalone film.

00:00:11.940 --> 00:00:16.340
Larson has been hard at work in Hollywood
since she was just a kid, and offscreen, she's

00:00:16.340 --> 00:00:18.600
known for being candidly outspoken and down
to earth.

00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:22.780
It turns out that her real-life story is just
as fascinating as those of the characters

00:00:22.780 --> 00:00:23.780
she portrays.

00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:27.859
Larson was very young when she already knew

00:00:27.860 --> 00:00:29.240
what she wanted to do with her life.

00:00:29.649 --> 00:00:32.180
"I told my mom I knew what my Dharma was..

00:00:32.180 --> 00:00:33.519
When I was 6 years old.

00:00:33.520 --> 00:00:35.700
Your Dharma was... so… hippie parents."

00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:40.140
Hippie or not, her parents took their daughter's
ambitions seriously, and it's easy to understand

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:44.230
why: that year, she became the youngest student
ever accepted at San Francisco's American

00:00:44.230 --> 00:00:45.360
Conservatory Theater.

00:00:45.460 --> 00:00:49.500
Larson's mother soon relocated the aspiring
actress and her younger sister to Los Angeles,

00:00:49.500 --> 00:00:54.180
where the trio squeezed into a tiny furnished
studio apartment and Larson started auditioning.

00:00:54.280 --> 00:00:58.500
Of her mother's support, Larson told Elle:
"My mom always felt like she was a creative

00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:01.360
person and her parents wouldn't allow her
to have that kind of life.

00:01:01.700 --> 00:01:05.340
So when I got interested in acting, she was
like, 'Okay, let's make this happen.'"

00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:09.540
And things did start to happen: At the age
of seven, she filmed commercial parodies for

00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:10.940
The Tonight Show with Jay Leno.

00:01:11.300 --> 00:01:13.760
"Thanks, roadkill EZ Bake Oven!"

00:01:14.020 --> 00:01:18.119
After that, Larson started to book small parts
on TV shows including Popular and Touched

00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:18.940
By an Angel.

00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:23.600
Despite her early successes, Larson's career

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:25.060
wasn't a straight climb to the top.

00:01:25.229 --> 00:01:28.080
She faced plenty of rejection while trying
to land her breakout role.

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:31.740
It was emotionally taxing for the young star,
not to mention the financial toll it took

00:01:31.740 --> 00:01:32.240
on her.

00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:35.460
She told the Guardian:
"I'd hit the point where I'd go, 'OK, that's

00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:35.960
it.

00:01:35.960 --> 00:01:36.740
That's the last dollar.

00:01:36.740 --> 00:01:38.460
I've got to quit or go to college or whatever.'

00:01:38.460 --> 00:01:42.039
But that day or the next day, something would
happen that would make it strangely apparent

00:01:42.040 --> 00:01:44.109
I'm supposed to keep doing this a bit longer."

00:01:44.360 --> 00:01:48.640
For Larson, the turnaround started when she
was cast as Kate on United States of Tara,

00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:51.560
and drew critical praise for her performance
as the sardonic teenager.

00:01:51.560 --> 00:01:55.280
By the time the show was canceled after three
seasons, Larson's star was on the rise and

00:01:55.280 --> 00:01:57.740
casting directors had started taking note.

00:01:58.300 --> 00:01:59.720
"Wanna take a picture with me?"

00:02:02.540 --> 00:02:04.520
Of all the movies she's made, Larson has said

00:02:04.520 --> 00:02:09.140
there's only one she re-watches: the wacky
action comedy Scott Pilgrim vs. the World.

00:02:09.140 --> 00:02:13.590
Unfortunately, her turn as ex-girlfriend Envy
Adams went largely unnoticed by theatergoers

00:02:13.590 --> 00:02:15.000
during its 2010 release.

00:02:15.140 --> 00:02:17.240
Still, things were slowly picking up for Larson.

00:02:17.380 --> 00:02:21.300
She had better luck in her supporting role
as Jonah Hill's love interest in 21 Jump Street,

00:02:21.300 --> 00:02:25.140
which was not only critically well-received,
it was a hit at the box office as well, taking

00:02:25.140 --> 00:02:27.360
in more than $200 million worldwide.

00:02:27.540 --> 00:02:31.440
Larson garnered praise from critics as a standout
who brought nuance and depth to a supporting

00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:33.740
character that could have been totally unmemorable.

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:38.400
She may have only been 23 when she stepped

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:43.160
into her first leading role as troubled youth
care worker Grace in the 2013 indie drama

00:02:43.160 --> 00:02:47.810
Short Term 12, but Larson had been working
hard for more than 15 years by the time she

00:02:47.810 --> 00:02:49.480
got her first major big-screen break.

00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:54.280
Larson shadowed real workers and did extensive
research to capture Grace's emotions with

00:02:54.280 --> 00:02:55.440
depth and accuracy.

00:02:55.750 --> 00:02:58.380
She told New York magazine:
"I was terrified.

00:02:58.380 --> 00:02:59.040
And excited.

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:03.000
It was confusing: When we screened the film
at the South by Southwest festival for the

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:05.940
first time, I had never had people look at
me that way before."

00:03:06.330 --> 00:03:10.220
The film won the Narrative Feature award at
South by Southwest and Larson took home a

00:03:10.220 --> 00:03:11.860
Gotham Award at the end of the year.

00:03:14.520 --> 00:03:16.780
Larson isn't all about drama all the time.

00:03:16.940 --> 00:03:21.750
2013 also saw a pair of rom-com supporting
roles in The Spectacular Now and Don Jon.

00:03:21.920 --> 00:03:26.720
And in 2015, she charmed audiences with her
comedic chops in Amy Schumer's Trainwreck,

00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:31.320
playing a fictionalized version of Schumer's
sister Kim, and enduring a very unusual audition

00:03:31.320 --> 00:03:32.480
process to land the part.

00:03:32.720 --> 00:03:36.840
It all started when director Judd Apatow called
Larson and invited her to lunch.

00:03:36.840 --> 00:03:38.600
"My agent was like what, what happened?

00:03:38.600 --> 00:03:41.740
What did you do, I was like I don't know,
I just talked about myself for a while and

00:03:41.740 --> 00:03:45.280
I ate a salad and I was like - well what did
they want and I was like I DON'T KNOW!"

00:03:45.680 --> 00:03:50.220
As she tells it, Larson came back for lunch
every day, and soon Bill Hader showed up too.

00:03:50.220 --> 00:03:53.890
Apatow eventually filled the actors in on
his plans, using the get-togethers as part

00:03:53.890 --> 00:03:55.940
of his unorthodox casting process.

00:03:58.500 --> 00:04:00.560
After reading Emma Donoghue's book Room in

00:04:00.580 --> 00:04:04.860
a single day, Larson knew she wanted to play
the part of Ma, the kidnapped teen turned

00:04:04.860 --> 00:04:06.520
long-term captive and mother.

00:04:06.520 --> 00:04:10.220
After beating out several other actresses
for the part, Larson dove into the role with

00:04:10.220 --> 00:04:11.540
a massive amount of prep work.

00:04:11.540 --> 00:04:16.739
She avoided the sun to achieve a pallid complexion,
packed on 15 pounds of lean muscle and even

00:04:16.739 --> 00:04:21.039
stayed home for a month, an experience she
admitted was intensely draining both physically

00:04:21.040 --> 00:04:22.020
and emotionally.

00:04:22.100 --> 00:04:26.800
Larson's emotionally raw performance in Room
was met with near-universal acclaim, and earned

00:04:26.800 --> 00:04:29.580
her the biggest Hollywood accolade of them
all: an Oscar.

00:04:32.260 --> 00:04:35.100
The claustrophobic confines of Room were followed

00:04:35.100 --> 00:04:40.040
with a complete 180 for Larson when she landed
a main role in 2017's Kong: Skull Island.

00:04:40.110 --> 00:04:45.660
Shot in Vietnam and Hawaii, it was full of
big everything big budget, big A-list co-stars,

00:04:45.660 --> 00:04:48.340
big special effects, and of course one very
big gorilla.

00:04:48.480 --> 00:04:52.760
While her first post-Oscar performance wasn't
received with the glowing critical praise

00:04:52.770 --> 00:04:57.169
as past roles, she was was credited with turning
the franchise's traditionally fainting female

00:04:57.169 --> 00:05:00.960
lead into a more modern, empowered counterpart
to Tom Hiddleston's explorer.

00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:05.330
More importantly, Kong: Skull Island proved
Larson had the star power to carry a popcorn

00:05:05.330 --> 00:05:11.700
flick: it was her most successful box office
outing to date, taking in $566 million worldwide.

00:05:14.300 --> 00:05:16.820
At Comic-Con 2016, the cast of the Marvel

00:05:16.820 --> 00:05:21.500
Universe assembled on stage for a family photo,
and then came a special announcement: Brie

00:05:21.500 --> 00:05:26.580
Larson was introduced as Captain Marvel a
role everyone at Marvel takes seriously.

00:05:26.940 --> 00:05:30.039
"You're going to get the most powerful superhero
that we've ever, that we've introduced and

00:05:30.040 --> 00:05:33.500
I think one of the most unique and inspiring
origin stories."

00:05:33.600 --> 00:05:37.919
That's an awful lot of responsibility for
one movie, but Larson seems eager to step

00:05:37.920 --> 00:05:38.919
up to the challenge.

00:05:39.000 --> 00:05:42.300
She told reporters:
"I'm really proud of what it is that we're

00:05:42.310 --> 00:05:43.310
making.

00:05:43.310 --> 00:05:45.280
I think all the hype and anticipation will
be worth it."

00:05:45.760 --> 00:05:46.760
Looks like a perfect fit.

